/-! ### `hknot`, `hP₁ hP₂`, `hQ₁ hQ₂`, `hK`

The six tracks of the subdivision, in both orientations.  Four of them are single edges;
the two tracks for the opposite pair `{0,1}`, `{2,3}` of `K₄`-edges pass through `y₂ = 5` and
`x₂ = 2`.

**17.5, claim (5).**  In the paper's one-based indexing this is `h>1`;
with Lean's zero-based indices it is `0<h`.

PAPER: *"(5) `h>1`."*

The properties the printed proof requires of `A`:

* `A ⊆ A₁`;
* `A` is connected;
* `x₀, x₁, x₂` all have neighbours in `A`;
* every vertex in `Y` that is nonadjacent to `x₂` has a neighbour in `A`;
* **and** (see the module header) `y` has a neighbour in `A`.

The paper's step *"Take a minimal track in `H \ {c₁,c₂}` between `a₁,a₂`;
then by the hypothesis of the theorem this track has odd length, and so `c₁,c₂` have
opposite biparity."*

The bookkeeping behind *"By reversing `S₂` we may assume that `S₁` and `S₂` agree on `T₁`;
and we may assume they disagree on `T₂`"*: once **some** pair of indices carries a twist, and
every index is either an agreement or a disagreement, **every** index has a twist partner.

The five-by-five case analysis on the local types of `X₁` and `X₂`, using claims (2), (3)
and the endgame.  Six of the twenty-five cases need the path traversed the other way round;
that is why the disjunction on the right is there.
